#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# find_peaks

#% text
# `find_peaks(data)` gives the peaks of a list of real values as pairs `[position, value]`.

#% section
# Details

#% item
# A peak is a run of equal values above its neighbours; a run at an end of the list needs only its inner neighbour. Its position is the middle of the run.

#% item
# `find_peaks(data, sigma)` first blurs the data by a sampled Gaussian of width `sigma` (padding the ends by reflection) and reports the original peaks nearest to the peaks of the blurred data; `sigma` 0 (the default) does not blur.

#% item
# `find_peaks(data, sigma, s)` keeps the peaks with sharpness above `s` (the negated second difference, default 0); `find_peaks(data, sigma, s, t)` those with value above `t`.

#% item
# `data` may also be a list of pairs `[x, y]`; the positions are then values of `x`.

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% cell
find_peaks([1, 3, 5, 6, 6, 4, 3, 2, 4, 7, 3, 2, 4, 2, 2, 1])
#% output
#= [[9/2, 6], [10, 7], [13, 4]]

#% cell
v = [2, 1, 3, 5, 6, 6, 4, 3, 2, 4, 7, 3, 4, 2, 2, 1]

#% cell
find_peaks(v, 1)
#% output
#= [[11/2, 6], [11, 7]]

#% cell
find_peaks(v, 0, 3)
#% output
#= [[11, 7]]

#% cell
find_peaks(v, 0, 0, 5.1)
#% output
#= [[11/2, 6], [11, 7]]

#% section
# See also

#% text
# `max`, `ordering`, `differences`
